  "narrative": "No independent validation budget remains, so all due results are assessed as discovery-only 95% Welch intervals with explicit caution for multiplicity and post-hoc selection. Direction convention is as stated: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate corresponds to the claimed stronger-dependency pattern (raw exposed-minus-comparator negative), and I do not re-sign intervals.\n\nThe new feature_063 paired-context interaction tests do not establish a new threshold-supported effect-modification claim in the clean core-negative eligibility population. H247 (feature_053>=10 modifier, signed +0.086 [-0.076, 0.248]) and H249 (feature_051>=2.6735 modifier, signed +0.104 [-0.053, 0.260]) are positive but far below the 0.15-unit threshold and cross zero. H250 (feature_053 modifier within high feature_040, signed +0.168 [-0.056, 0.393]) has a point estimate above threshold but the lower bound is negative and the interval includes zero. H251 (feature_040 modifier within high feature_051, signed +0.268 [0.047, 0.489]) is the most suggestive: it excludes zero and has a point estimate above threshold, but its lower bound is well below 0.15 and the contrast is small-sample and post-hoc. H252 (feature_051 modifier within high feature_053, signed +0.121 [-0.078, 0.320]) is below threshold and crosses zero. These are kept unresolved and deferred because they may represent weak, context-dependent interaction signals, but none meet the threshold criterion of a signed lower bound exceeding 0.15. They do not justify stronger claims of feature_063 effect modification, especially after repeated high-stratum selection and overlapping feature_040/feature_051/feature_053 contexts.\n\nThe feature_003 results remain narrow and subthreshold. H253 (feature_003 within core-negative, feature_063=0, feature_053>=10) is positive but below threshold (+0.115 [0.045, 0.185]), so I keep it unresolved and close it as a weak directional context rather than a meaningful association. H254 (feature_053>=10 interaction for feature_003 in the same eligible population) is signed negative (-0.124 [-0.242, -0.007]), i.e. it is in the opposite direction from the claimed stronger-dependency interaction and excludes zero in that opposite direction; this does not support the H254 claim, so I reject it and close it. H93 (feature_003 among feature_002=0, signed +0.142 [0.064, 0.221]) is positive but below threshold, so it remains a weak unresolved directional signal. H94 (feature_003 among feature_002=1, signed +0.039 [-0.112, 0.190]) is essentially null, so it is rejected.\n\nThe categorical contrasts are also null or unsupported. H165 (rare feature_059 level_002 vs level_003, signed -0.023 [-0.300, 0.253]) is not in the claimed direction and is too wide to support a threshold claim. H166 (feature_061 level_001 vs level_002, signed +0.057 [-0.024, 0.138]) and H167 (feature_061 level_003 vs level_002, signed -0.007 [-0.150, 0.137]) are near zero, with H166's upper bound below threshold and H167 mixed. I reject them as threshold claims and close them.\n\nOverall, the new due evidence does not add any accepted threshold predictor or subgroup association. It leaves feature_063 as a context-dependent marker with several accepted mean-difference contexts but no new accepted interaction, and feature_003 remains a weak, subthreshold marker at best. No causal, biological, coding-level, single-gene-knockout, or external-generalizability claim is warranted from these results.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 20 appraise update. Dataset-only threshold criterion: accept only if signed 95% Welch lower bound exceeds 0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction. No validation requests remain, so all new claims are discovery-only and multiplicity-limited.\n\nMain robust accepted associations remain: feature_027=1 stronger dependency, feature_037=1 stronger dependency, feature_002=1 weaker dependency, and feature_010=1 weaker dependency, with multiple accepted subgroup/persistence analyses. Key validation-robust claims include H128, H24, H63, and H37.\n\nfeature_063 remains context-dependent. It is not uniformly threshold precise overall. In the clean core-negative eligibility (feature_002=0, feature_037=0, feature_010=0, feature_027=0), feature_063=1 has threshold-supported mean-difference associations in several selected high numeric or combined strata, including H213 and accepted high numeric contexts such as H233, H234, H225, H214, H236, H237, H245. However, the new paired-context interaction tests did not establish that the accepted high-stratum effects are genuinely threshold-supported effect modification. H247 +0.086 [-0.076,0.248] and H249 +0.104 [-0.053,0.260] are weak positive interactions but below threshold. H250 +0.168 [-0.056,0.393], H251 +0.268 [0.047,0.489], and H252 +0.121 [-0.078,0.320] remain unresolved/deferred as directional or uncertain interactions; none has lower bound above threshold. These small-cell, post-hoc paired-context interactions should not be treated as established modifiers of feature_063 in this dataset.\n\nfeature_003 remains a narrow, weak positive signal and not a broad threshold predictor. H253 +0.115 [0.045,0.185] and H93 +0.142 [0.064,0.221] are below threshold, so they are closed as unresolved directional context rather than meaningful effects. H254 is rejected because its signed interaction estimate is negative and in the opposite direction from the claimed stronger-dependency interaction. H94 is null.\n\nCategorical contrasts: feature_059 level_002 vs level_003 (H165), feature_061 level_001 vs level_002 (H166), and feature_061 level_003 vs level_002 (H167) are null/unsupported and closed as rejected. Rare cells and wide intervals limit interpretation, but none supports a threshold association.\n\nResidual uncertainty: whether feature_063 high-numeric accepted mean-difference contexts reflect true additive/effect-modification structure or selected narrow contexts; but available interaction tests are subthreshold. No further validation can be requested. Do not make causal, biological, coding-level, single-gene-knockout, or generalizability claims."
